  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nQuiet and dry conditions will persist across the region as high \npressure remains dominant. Temperatures will continue to trend near \n10 degrees higher than climatological normals. FireWx concerns will \npersist through this evening as drought conditions continue. \nIncreasing moisture Friday should serve to mitigate the FireWx \nconcerns; however, ongoing drought conditions will only be partially \nalleviated by upcoming rainfall this weekend. Continue to follow the \nguidance of local and state fire officials and adhere to any active \nburn bans./KP/\n\nAn upper shortwave will shift from the Southern Plains into the \nMid MS Valley over the weekend. Increasing mid level moisture will\nresult in increasing clouds by Friday, with virga likely \nbeginning to overspread the area by Friday afternoon as this \nmoisture encounters drier low levels. By early Saturday morning, \nshowers will be possible mainly around the ArkLaMiss/ Delta area. \nAcross the remainder of the area, scattered showers may mostly \nhold off until more into Saturday afternoon/evening, continuing \ninto Sunday. Thunderstorms are also possible Saturday into Sunday,\nbut no severe weather is expected. Given the scattered nature of \nprecip, many outdoor plans may still proceed without disruption \nthis weekend, but it would be a good idea to keep close watch on \nconditions and/or have a backup plan in case rain does occur at \nyour location.\n\nA break in rain chances is still expected Monday as shortwave\nridging shifts across the area, then a deepening upper trough\nlooks to bring a more organized storm system to the area for the\nTuesday/Wednesday time frame. With this system, it appears more\nprobable showers and thunderstorms would be organized along a cold\nfront pushing eastward across the area. While instability with\nthis system is uncertain, stronger deep layer shear appears\nlikely, which could result in some strong storms. With this \nsystem still several days away, it is too early to have much \nconfidence in timing/threat details. /DL/",
